### Problem:
You are working on a Vulkan graphics application and need to manage image memory barriers for efficient resource synchronization. Your task is to implement a function that takes in a set of image memory barriers and organizes them to ensure proper synchronization between different stages of the graphics pipeline.

You are given a code snippet that demonstrates the creation of image memory barriers using the Vulkan API. The `ImagePreBarrier` and `ImagePostBarrier` vectors are used to store the pre- and post-barriers, respectively. Each barrier is defined by specifying the access flags, initial and final image layouts, queue family ownership, image handle, and image subresource range.

Your task is to implement a function `organizeImageMemoryBarriers` that takes in the `ImagePreBarrier` and `ImagePostBarrier` vectors and returns a new vector `OrganizedBarriers` that contains the organized set of image memory barriers. The organization should ensure that the barriers are correctly ordered to maintain proper synchronization between different pipeline stages.

You should consider the following rules for organizing the barriers:
1. Barriers with the same image handle should be grouped together.
2. Within each group, the pre-barriers should be ordered before the post-barriers.
3. If there are multiple pre-barriers or post-barriers for the same image handle, they should be ordered based on their subresource range, with lower mip levels and array layers coming before higher ones.

Implement the function `organizeImageMemoryBarriers` as per the given rules.

```cpp
#include <vulkan/vulkan.hpp>

std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier> organizeImageMemoryBarriers(
    const std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier>& ImagePreBarrier,
    const std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier>& ImagePostBarrier) {
  // Your implementation here
}
```

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <vulkan/vulkan.hpp>
#include <map>

std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier> organizeImageMemoryBarriers(
    const std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier>& ImagePreBarrier,
    const std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier>& ImagePostBarrier) {
  std::map<vk::Image, std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier>> barrierMap;

  // Group pre-barriers by image handle
  for (const auto& preBarrier : ImagePreBarrier) {
    barrierMap[preBarrier.image].emplace_back(preBarrier);
  }

  // Group post-barriers by image handle
  for (const auto& postBarrier : ImagePostBarrier) {
    barrierMap[postBarrier.image].emplace_back(postBarrier);
  }

  std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier> OrganizedBarriers;

  // Process barriers for each image handle
  for (const auto& entry : barrierMap) {
    const auto& image = entry.first;
    const auto& barriers = entry.second;

    // Sort pre-barriers based on subresource range
    std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier> preBarriers;
    // Sort post-barriers based on subresource range
    std::vector<vk::ImageMemoryBarrier> postBarriers;

    for (const auto& barrier : barriers) {
      if (barrier.srcAccessMask == vk::AccessFlagBits::eMemoryWrite) {
        preBarriers.emplace_back(barrier);
      } else {
        postBarriers.emplace_back(barrier);
      }
    }

    std::sort(preBarriers.begin(), preBarriers.end(), [](const auto& a, const auto& b) {
      return a.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel < b.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel ||
             (a.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel == b.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel &&
              a.subresourceRange.baseArrayLayer < b.subresourceRange.baseArrayLayer);
    });

    std::sort(postBarriers.begin(), postBarriers.end(), [](const auto& a, const auto& b) {
      return a.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel < b.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel ||
             (a.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel == b.subresourceRange.baseMipLevel &&
              a.subresourceRange.baseArrayLayer < b.subresourceRange.baseArrayLayer);
    });

    // Append sorted pre-barriers and post-barriers to the organized list
    OrganizedBarriers.insert(OrganizedBarriers.end(), preBarriers.begin(), preBarriers.end());
    OrganizedBarriers.insert(OrganizedBarriers.end(), postBarriers.begin(), postBarriers.end());
  }

  return OrganizedBarriers;
}
```

The `organizeImageMemoryBarriers` function first groups the pre- and post-barriers by image handle using a map. Then, for each image handle, it sorts the pre-barriers and post-barriers based on their subresource range and appends them to the `OrganizedBarriers` vector. This ensures that the barriers are correctly ordered for proper synchronization between different pipeline stages.

