### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a program that processes JSON data representing responses from a survey. The program should read the JSON data, deserialize it into a struct, perform some calculations, and then serialize the results back into JSON.

You are given a Rust code snippet that defines a struct `Response` using the `serde` library for serialization and deserialization. Your task is to complete the program by implementing the necessary functions to process the survey responses.

The `Response` struct has the following fields:
- `id: u64` (represents the response ID)
- `answers: Vec<u8>` (represents the answers to the survey questions as a list of unsigned 8-bit integers)

You need to implement the following functions:
1. `read_responses_from_json`: This function takes a file path as input and reads the JSON data from the file, deserializes it into a vector of `Response` structs, and returns the vector.
2. `calculate_average_score`: This function takes a reference to the vector of `Response` structs as input and calculates the average score for each question in the survey. The average score for each question is the sum of all responses to that question divided by the total number of responses.
3. `write_results_to_json`: This function takes the calculated average scores as a reference to a vector of floats and writes them to a JSON file.

You should assume that the JSON data is well-formed and matches the structure of the `Response` struct.

Complete the program by implementing the functions to process the survey responses as described.

### Solution:
```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::prelude::*;

#[derive(Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Response {
    id: u64,
    answers: Vec<u8>,
}

fn read_responses_from_json(file_path: &str) -> Result<Vec<Response>, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let file = File::open(file_path)?;
    let responses: Vec<Response> = serde_json::from_reader(file)?;
    Ok(responses)
}

fn calculate_average_score(responses: &Vec<Response>) -> Vec<f64> {
    let num_questions = responses[0].answers.len();
    let mut total_scores = vec![0; num_questions];
    for response in responses {
        for (i, &answer) in response.answers.iter().enumerate() {
            total_scores[i] += answer as usize;
        }
    }
    total_scores.iter().map(|&score| score as f64 / responses.len() as f64).collect()
}

fn write_results_to_json(average_scores: &Vec<f64>, file_path: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let file = File::create(file_path)?;
    serde_json::to_writer(file, average_scores)?;
    Ok(())
}

fn main() {
    let responses = read_responses_from_json("survey_responses.json").unwrap();
    let average_scores = calculate_average_score(&responses);
    write_results_to_json(&average_scores, "average_scores.json").unwrap();
}
```

### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a function that takes in an array of objects representing items and generates an HTML list based on the object properties. Each object in the array will have the following properties: `title`, `description`, `updated_at`, and `created_at`. Your function should generate an unordered list (UL) with list items (LI) for each object property, using the values from the objects.

Write a function `generateHTMLList` that takes an array of objects and returns a string containing the HTML list.

Function Signature: `function generateHTMLList(items: object[]): string`

Example:
Input:
```javascript
const items = [
  {
    title: "Item 1",
    description: "Description for Item 1",
    updated_at: "2022-01-15",
    created_at: "2022-01-10"
  },
  {
    title: "Item 2",
    description: "Description for Item 2",
    updated_at: "2022-01-20",
    created_at: "2022-01-18"
  }
];
generateHTMLList(items);
```
Output:
```html
<ul>
  <li>Item 1</li>
  <li>Description for Item 1</li>
  <li>2022-01-15</li>
  <li>2022-01-10</li>
  <li>Item 2</li>
  <li>Description for Item 2</li>
  <li>2022-01-20</li>
  <li>2022-01-18</li>
</ul>
```

### Solution:
```javascript
function generateHTMLList(items) {
  let htmlList = "<ul>\n";
  items.forEach(item => {
    htmlList += `<li>${item.title}</li>\n`;
    htmlList += `<li>${item.description}</li>\n`;
    htmlList += `<li>${item.updated_at}</li>\n`;
    htmlList += `<li>${item.created_at}</li>\n`;
  });
  htmlList += "</ul>";
  return htmlList;
}
```

